<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arkansas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arkansas is a state of the south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a>, with its capital at Little Rock. Its south-west was part of the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caddo">Caddo</a>, and the region passed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> of 1803. Admitted to the Union as a slave state in 1836, it seceded and joined the Confederacy in May 1861.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arkansas.jpg" alt="Arkansas" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Arkansas.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The territory they came to control, known in Spanish as Comanchería, stretched from the upper <b>Arkansas</b> River to the Balcones Escarpment near San Antonio and from the Cross Timbers to the Pecos River.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Lincoln's call for 75,000 militia followed on 15 April, and Virginia, <b>Arkansas</b>, Tennessee and North Carolina seceded between April and May.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the next two centuries the new way of life spread up the Mississippi to Wisconsin, where Aztalan, a palisaded town with platform mounds on the Crawfish River, was occupied until the 13th century, and through the South-East from the <b>Arkansas</b> River to Georgia and Florida.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1957 President Dwight D. Eisenhower sent paratroopers to escort nine Black students into Central High School in Little Rock, <b>Arkansas</b>, after Governor Orval Faubus had used the National Guard to bar them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>…States the doctrine of states’ rights was invoked to defend slavery and then the racial segregation of the southern states, and federal troops had to enforce the Supreme Court’s ruling against segregated schools in Brown v. Board of Education of 1954, as at Little Rock, <b>Arkansas</b>, in 1957.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>The tune also carried many other words: the Civil War &quot;Marching Song of the First <b>Arkansas</b>&quot; of Black soldiers, Mark Twain's satire on the Philippine–American War in 1901, the labour song &quot;Solidarity Forever&quot;, college fight songs, children's parodies, and football chants that began at Tottenham Hotspur in 1961…</blockquote><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a></li></ul></section>
